### What was deleted

Four files, and this is the one change in this tree that is not a guard.
`attention/scheduler.cuh`, `exception.h`, `allocator.h` and
`attention/heap.h` contained no device code at all -- no `__global__` body, no
`__device__` function, 2,138 lines and 97,847 bytes of host C++ between them.
Every one was already guarded in its ENTIRETY, so NVRTC had always seen four
empty files; the guards were doing a deletion's work at the cost of shipping
the text, and `kernels/` is device text only.

Each had a Rust owner before it was deleted, which is why this is a deletion
and not a port: `src/plan/{decode,prefill,mla,sm90}.rs` for the scheduler,
`src/plan/error.rs`, `src/plan/alloc.rs` and `src/plan/heap.rs` for the other
three, 3,853 lines of Rust that were written against these files and had been
carrying the behaviour for some time.

The measurement that made it safe: `scheduler.cuh` was `#include`d by nothing
in this tree, and `allocator.h` and `heap.h` only by `scheduler.cuh`. Walking
the four compilation roots reached 40 of the 43 internalised files and missed
exactly those three. `exception.h` was the fourth and the only reachable one
-- `page.cuh` and `utils.cuh` included it -- so deleting it deleted two
`#include` lines as well. That is safe for a reason worth stating plainly:
because `exception.h` was guarded in its entirety, `FLASHINFER_CHECK` and
`FLASHINFER_ERROR` were ALREADY undefined in every NVRTC translation unit
here, and the expansions that survive are all inside `#define` bodies NVRTC
never expands or host launchers it never reaches. What NVRTC sees did not
change by one token. (Removing the launchers, below, took the second kind
with them: 13 expansions remain and every one is a `#define` continuation
line.)

### And then the host C++ inside the surviving files went too

Four whole files was the easy half. The rule is *the device tree holds pure
kernels*,
and after those four deletions it held in effect and not in letter: 34 guarded
regions carrying 1,503 lines of upstream host text were still on disk, hidden
from NVRTC by `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__` and by XQA's own `#ifndef
GENERATE_CUBIN`, plus **1,055 lines in 13 `cudaError_t` host launchers that
were not guarded at all** -- `SinglePrefillWithKVCacheDispatched`,
`BatchDecodeWithPagedKVCacheDispatched` and their siblings across
`cascade.cuh`, `decode.cuh`, `mla.cuh` and `prefill.cuh`. Those thirteen
survived only by being uninstantiated templates, which is a far weaker shield
than a guard: a single explicit instantiation anywhere would have handed
`cudaMalloc`, `std::vector` and `cudaStreamCreate` to a device compiler. They
form a closed set -- the only callers of any of the thirteen are the others --
so removing them removed no reachable code.

A fifth whole file went with them: **`xqa/hostUtils.h`**, one `inline
cudaLaunchConfig_t makeLaunchConfig(...)`, no device text, included only from
the `mha.cuh` host block being deleted. It was found by the deletion rather
than before it.

Net across both trees: **15 files touched, 2,694 lines and 123,642 bytes
removed**, each site keeping a `// PIE: REMOVED` marker naming what stood
there -- 40 markers, and they are inside that net. `MODIFICATIONS` carries the
