### What is NOT FlashInfer's, and no longer sits next to it

The six small headers PIE wrote to stand in for names NVRTC does not ship --

    cstdint, type_traits, cuda.h, cuda_runtime.h, bit,
    boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp

-- used to share a directory with the FlashInfer text, which made this a
paragraph about disambiguation. They are now in `shim/`, a directory that
holds nothing else and whose README states the rule, so the disambiguation is
structural and this paragraph is only history -- `bit` and
`boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp` have since been retired, so four of the six
named above are live. `shim/README.md` is the register, and it records
each addition and retirement rather than restating a total -- which is why
no count of the directory appears here either.

They are named after the headers they stand in for because an `#include`
resolves against a header set by exact spelling -- this crate has no include
path on disk, so the spelling in the directive *is* the identity of the
header. Each one exists because guarding its include was tried and measured to
break device code, and each says so in its own banner. In particular
`boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp` contained no Boost code and was not derived from
Boost: it was a one-line constexpr absolute value under a name FlashInfer
asks for, and it is written in the past tense because its only asker,
`fp16.h`, is deleted and it went with it.

### Where it lives, and why it moved

`kernels/flashinfer/`, subtree shape exactly as upstream ships it, plus
`LICENSE` and `MODIFICATIONS` at its root. It was `csrc/vendor/flashinfer/`,
then `csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/`.

The first move is the point, not a side effect: this crate compiles every
kernel through NVRTC, so its device tree is device text and nothing else, and
a directory called `vendor` said the opposite -- that the text was a guest
with its own rules. It is not a guest. It is compiled by the same JIT, guarded
by the same markers, and tested by the same tests as everything beside it.

The second move took it out from under `attn/`, and that is a correction
rather than a preference: `flashinfer/comm/` holds the two all-reduce headers
that are `kernels_cuda::comm`'s NVRTC root, and an all-reduce is not
attention. Under `attn/` the path made a claim about the text that two of its
files contradicted. At `kernels/flashinfer/` it makes none -- the directory
says what the text IS and not which of our families happens to use it, which
is also why there is no `vendor/` around it to patrol. Provenance is carried
by the name, by the banner in every file, by `LICENSE` and by this document;
none of those needs a boundary.

**Not one byte of the twenty-nine changed in the move** -- all 28 source files
as the tree then stood, and `LICENSE`, zero changed lines each; only
`MODIFICATIONS`, which is PIE's
own file, was edited, to record the new path. The subtree shape is what makes
that true: upstream's files include each other relatively (`"../cp_async.cuh"`,
`"mask.cuh"`) and a relative spelling survives a move that carries the whole
tree. Four of those 28 were deleted afterwards and for an unrelated reason;
that is "What was deleted" above, not part of the move.

Seventeen `#include` directives changed across the whole internalisation, and
the split is worth stating because only one half of it touches upstream text:
**twelve in PIE's own files** -- ten `<flashinfer/attention/…>` and two
`<xqa/…>`, which is where an angled, tree-rooted spelling lived -- and **five
inside the XQA files themselves**, which had already been made tree-relative
for an unrelated reason (see the XQA section below) and simply gained the new
prefix. No FlashInfer file contains a changed directive.


