CCCL, NVIDIA CUDA headers
-------------------------

Neither is carried, and that is the point of `shim/`. No toolkit text is
in this crate's binary and none is redistributed: every file under `shim/`
is written here, and nothing was copied out of `$CUDA_HOME` or out of a CCCL
checkout to get there.

Two probes used to check that claim by READING the real headers at run time,
to separate "is this source acceptable to NVRTC" from "is our shim correct".
`examples/flashinfer_probe.rs` read FlashInfer's own CCCL checkout and went at
`1a08b179a` with sixteen other examples; `examples/halftype_parity.rs` opened
NVIDIA's `cuda_fp16.h` and `cuda_bf16.h` from `$CUDA_HOME`, and went with the
rest of `examples/`. Neither ever carried what it read -- a probe compares, it
does not copy -- so their going changes nothing this notice has to declare. It
does mean the paragraph above is now stated rather than measured, which is
worth saying plainly: nothing carried has a `cccl` or `cutlass` path, and
`shim/` holds no file under either, but no probe re-checks that per run.


The LICENSE file
----------------

`kernels/flashinfer/LICENSE` is the stock Apache License 2.0 text, 230 lines,
as upstream ships it. It is the licence *of its own directory*, because a
rebase replaces a directory and not a crate -- which is why there were two of
these while `kernels/xqa/` was carried, byte-identical to this one and kept
separate anyway. That one went with its directory; this one stays with its
own. It may not be renamed: the carried header set is walked from disk by
`build.rs`, which excludes a file by NAME (`LICENSE`, `MODIFICATIONS`,
`README.md`), so a rename would put 12 KB of Apache-2.0 into the text NVRTC
copies per compile. The name stays plain so that "not device text" stays
obvious.
